/**
\page org_mitk_views_imagenavigator The Image Navigator

\imageMacro{imagenavigator-dox.svg,"Icon of the Image Navigator",2.00}

\imageMacroEx{QmtikImageNavigator_ImageNavigator.png,"The Image Navigator",8,384px}

The Image Navigator shows where the crosshair is and moves it to the position you enter: as a location in millimeters, as a slice of the axial, sagittal, and coronal render windows, and as a time step.
All render windows follow.
The view is disabled while no display is open.

\section org_mitk_views_imagenavigator_location Location in millimeters

The three fields in the "Loc. (mm)" row show the x, y, and z world coordinates of the crosshair in millimeters.
Type a value and press Enter, or use the arrows of a field, to move the crosshair along that axis.
Each field is limited to the extent of the data.

The frame of each field has the color of the render window whose slice it moves.
For data that is aligned with the world axes, x moves the sagittal slice, y the coronal slice, and z the axial slice.

\section org_mitk_views_imagenavigator_slices Slices

The "Axial", "Sagittal", and "Coronal" rows each have a slider and a number field for the slice that the render window of that direction shows.
Move the slider or enter a number to show that slice; the crosshair moves along.

Slices are counted from 0.
If the display is initialized with an image, the number is the image's voxel index in that direction, even where the render window shows the slices in the opposite order.

In a render window, the mouse wheel and the arrow up and down keys move through its slices as well.
A row is disabled when the display has no render window for its direction.

\section org_mitk_views_imagenavigator_time Time steps

The "Time step" row appears only when the data has more than one time step, for example for a dynamic series.
It selects the time step that all render windows show; time steps are counted from 0.
In a render window, the arrow left and right keys go to the previous and next time step.

The time step is common to all data.
Where images have different time steps, the Pixel Value view shows the time step of the image at the crosshair.

*/
